result:

incorrect! Ne8+ was the correct first move. Just look at the number of lines I calculated for nh5 vs ne8. There was a clear bias towards the move that "felt" more right, but ne8 was the correct move. I got the first few moves right but didn't expand my candidates towards the end of the line! when calculating you always need to expand candidate moves for every move, not just the first move. 

&nbsp;

takeaway: To avoid combinatorial explosion, only expand candidate moves at the root and when a forcing sequence ends with a quiet move; otherwise, keep your blinders on and calculate strictly down the tunnel of forced replies.} 1. Ne8+  (1. Nh5+  Kh7  (1... Kh8  )  (1... Kg8  2. Qh6 {Qh6 is a BS line because I forgot that d4 is still hanging} Qxd4+) 2. Qf6? {the line I thought was winning was just a draw} gxh5 3. g5 Kg8  (3... Qd8  4. g6+ Kh6 5. g7+) 4. g6 Bxh3 5. gxf7+ Kh7) Rxe8 2. Qf6+ Kh6 3. g5+  (3. Qf4+ {even cleaner is Qf4+, black will lose the rook and the game}  g5  (3... Kh7  4. Qxf7+ Kh6 5. Qxe8)  (3... Kg7  4. Qxf7+ Kh6 5. Qxe8 Qxd4+ 6. Kh1) 4. Qf6+ Kh7 5. Qxf7+ Kh8  (5... Kh6  6. Rf6#)) Kh5 {I saw all the way up to here and assumed Qc6+ was the only move} 4. Qg7! {[%cal Gg7h6]this is the move I didn't consider/missed. black's king is cut off from coming back to shelter}  (4. Qf3+ {this is the line I saw which is still actually winning if I found the Qf6 Rf4 idea}  Kxg5  (4... Kh4  5. Qf2+ Kxh3  (5... Kxg5 {if kxg5 then we go back to the original motif}  6. Qf6+ Kh5 7. Rf4) 6. Qh2+ Kg4 7. Rf4+ Kxg5 8. Qh4#) 5. Qf6+  (5. Qf4+ {this is the only move I considered in this line. but black manages to escape/gain counterplay}  Kh5 6. h4) Kh6 6. Rf4) Kxg5 {[%cal Gh3h4]} 5. Qf6+ Kh6  (5... Kh5  6. Rf4) 6. Rf4! {mate is now unstoppable without giving away the queen} Qb5 7. Rh4+ Qh5 8. Rxh5+ Kxh5 9. Qxf7 1-0

